{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795697893,"nonce":"Wf0SGkrEOIdo08+vcC4ZIGHBBGk=","title":"AOT: build an Android shared library for a NativeActivity APK","message":"Requested by the hxn session. An Android app with a NativeActivity is a shared library that the APK loads, lib\u003cname\u003e.so, and `ash --build` writes only executables.\n\nWanted: `ash --build libmain.so --target aarch64-linux-android main.hl --native-lib libxwindow.a ...`, and the same for x86_64-linux-android, with:\n\n- the same runtime, natives, Rust library merge and `xwindow_main` as the executable;\n- `ANativeActivity_onCreate`, from the android-activity crate in the window host's archive, exported and kept although nothing references it;\n- no `main` needed;\n- the NDK C++ runtime handled as for executables, so the library loads on its own in the APK with liblog and libandroid.\n\nA name that ends in .so or an explicit flag selects the library. hxn then packs it into an APK with a manifest for android.app.NativeActivity (lib_name main), the assets and a debug signature. Its next window host release exports `android_main`, which calls `xwindow_main`.\n\nAlready in place: AOT objects are built position-independent (target_abi.rs), Android programs get `xwindow_main` (llvm/aot_data.rs), archives are linked whole on Android, and the release ships static runtimes for aarch64-linux-android and x86_64-linux-android.\n\nTo work out: the link arguments (-shared, soname, keeping the entry through the Rust library merge and in the export table), what the runtime assumes about owning the process when it starts on the activity's thread (stack bounds for the collector, arguments, exit), and a test on an emulator. Related: 08bc732.","files":null}]}